WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:05.880
Ask people for blood donation, and you&nbsp;
might find many volunteers. Tell the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:10.200
same people that they'll get paid for&nbsp;
it, and many will now decline to help.

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:12.480
Why do you think that might be?

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:23.400
Self determination theory argues that we&nbsp;
do what we do, because we are motivated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:28.260
by three basic needs that drive our&nbsp;
behavior more than anything else.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:36.000
First comes autonomy. We desire to&nbsp;
have the freedom of making our own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.540
choices and not be forced to&nbsp;
do something we don’t want.

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:47.220
Second is competence. We want to feel&nbsp;
that we have the skills required to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:51.780
the work ourselves, and not be confronted&nbsp;
with tasks that we don’t understand.

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:59.220
Third is connection. We want to&nbsp;
experience a sense of belonging,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:03.780
of being needed, and not feel&nbsp;
useless or like an outsider.

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:10.980
We can think of motivation ranging from&nbsp;
“non-self-determined to self-determined.”&nbsp;

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:19.440
On the left we have amotivation, in the center&nbsp;
extrinsic motivation, and on the right intrinsic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:26.520
motivation. In terms of quality they range from&nbsp;
lower forms to higher forms. Along this spectrum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:33.480
are, according to self determination theory,&nbsp;
6 distinct types, represented here by: Anton,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:41.820
Mary, Taichi, Abeni, John, and Lalisa&nbsp;
who all have to study for a major exam.

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:51.000
Anton does not understand the topic and therefore&nbsp;
feels disconnected to the material. His need for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:57.540
competence remains unsatisfied and undermines&nbsp;
his autonomy — leading him to lose control over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:05.640
the situation. As a result he begins to think that&nbsp;
school is pointless. He experiences amotivation.

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:11.640
Mary likes to learn, when she&nbsp;
knows that if she does well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:15.960
she will be rewarded. But when no&nbsp;
one is around to stimulate her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:21.360
she feels disconnected. Mary is not&nbsp;
autonomous in her studies, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:27.420
she needs rewards that regulate her behavior&nbsp;
externally — a job her mother usually does.

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:32.880
This stage is called extrinsic&nbsp;
motivation: externally regulated

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:41.340
Taichi strives to win, or match the performance&nbsp;
of others. When he’s not among the top,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:47.160
his desires for competence and autonomy&nbsp;
are not satisfied. He feels guilty when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:52.920
he can’t be as good as others. The root&nbsp;
of his behavior is therefore external.

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:57.900
This stage is known as extrinsic&nbsp;
motivation: introjected regulation

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:07.440
Abeni values learning and sees herself as a good&nbsp;
student. To her, getting good grades is important,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:11.880
because it confirms her self-image.&nbsp;
Despite the fact that she doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:16.740
feel connected to the material, she does&nbsp;
well because she regulates her behavior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:23.100
by identifying with the idea of being a&nbsp;
good student. She’s motivated by an ideal.

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:29.280
This stage is called extrinsic motivation:&nbsp;
regulation through identification

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:37.680
John thinks learning is important, because it&nbsp;
makes him a better human being. To develop his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:42.360
intellect and become the best version of&nbsp;
himself, he tries to understand things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:48.600
even if they are boring. He feels connected&nbsp;
and competent. But since his behavior is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:54.300
regulated by the desire to live up to an&nbsp;
idea, he is still not fully autonomous.

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:59.880
This stage is known as extrinsic&nbsp;
motivation: integrated regulation

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:06.540
Lalisa learns things because&nbsp;
she is curious and enjoys it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:11.580
She can feel completely connected to the&nbsp;
material and often loses track of time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:19.260
Studying gives her a deep sense of satisfaction.&nbsp;
She experiences complete autonomy and as a result&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:23.820
of her intrinsic interests, develops&nbsp;
the highest forms of competence.

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:27.600
Now we speak of intrinsic motivation.

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:32.760
Regardless of where we are along the spectrum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:38.520
we all have complex human minds with&nbsp;
changing interests and conflicting desires.

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:45.060
Doing one thing, we may feel&nbsp;
fully motivated — autonomous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:48.720
competent and connected. But then, the next day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:55.680
life gets in the way and robs us of our three&nbsp;
basic needs — we feel nothing but amotivation.

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:01.980
To regain your self-determination,&nbsp;
you might want to take a break,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:06.000
seek a change in environment&nbsp;
or connect with other people.

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:13.380
Self-determination theory was developed&nbsp;
by the two American psychologists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:20.580
Richard Ryan and Edward Deci, in the 1970s.&nbsp;
More recent research points to some cultural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:27.960
differences. Many American students' seem to learn&nbsp;
to outcompete others. When Chinese study hard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:32.100
it’s often because they feel guilty&nbsp;
if they do not meet expectations.

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:40.620
On the interplay between extrinsic and&nbsp;
intrinsic motivation, Deci said: “Sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:46.440
money motivates, but that’s not the point. The&nbsp;
point is that while money is motivating people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:51.100
it is also undermining their&nbsp;
intrinsic motivation. ”

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:58.140
So what do you think about the model? Do&nbsp;
you agree or disagree with the theory? And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:03.180
what do you think about extrinsic rewards?&nbsp;
Share your thoughts in the comments below,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:06.060
and let us know what motivated you to do so.

